---
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import { SITE } from '../lib/site';

const title = 'About trendingprompt';
const description =
  'What trendingprompt publishes, how each prompt is tested before it goes up, and who is behind the site.';

const schema = {
  '@context': 'https://schema.org',
  '@type': 'AboutPage',
  name: title,
  description,
  url: `${SITE.url}/about`,
  publisher: { '@type': 'Organization', name: SITE.name, url: SITE.url, email: SITE.email },
};
---
<BaseLayout title={`${title} — trendingprompt`} description={description} schema={schema}>
  <section class="shell page">
    <p class="eyebrow">About</p>
    <h1 class="page__title">A prompt a day, with the picture it made</h1>
    <p class="lede">
      Most places that share AI prompts show you the image and bury the words, or list the words
      with no evidence they produce anything. trendingprompt publishes both, together, one entry
      at a time.
    </p>

    <div class="page__body prose">
      <h2>What gets published</h2>
      <p>
        One prompt a day. Each entry is a single generated image, the exact text that produced it,
        the tool it was made in, and — where it helps — notes on which clause is doing the heavy
        lifting and what to change for a different result. Nothing is paraphrased. The text on the
        page is the text that went into the model, punctuation and parameters included, so you can
        paste it and get something recognisably similar.
      </p>
      <p>
        Prompts are grouped by subject rather than by tool, because the useful part transfers.
        A lighting clause that works for a portrait in one model usually works in another; the
        parameters after the comma are the part you swap.
      </p>

      <h2>How each prompt is tested</h2>
      <p>
        A prompt does not go up until it has been run at least three times from a clean session,
        with no reference image and no prior context, to make sure the result is a property of the
        words rather than of a lucky seed. If two of three runs miss badly, the prompt gets rewritten
        or dropped. Where a result depends on a specific model version or parameter, that is stated
        on the entry — a prompt that only works on one release with one flag is a footnote, not a
        recipe.
      </p>
      <p>
        Images are the raw output of the tool named on the entry. When something has been upscaled
        or cropped, the entry says so. Nothing is retouched to look better than the prompt can
        actually produce, because the whole point of the site is that you can reproduce it.
      </p>

      <h2>The guides</h2>
      <p>
        Alongside the daily entries, the <a href="/blog">blog</a> covers the craft underneath them:
        how prompt structure affects what a model weights, what the parameters actually change,
        which camera and lighting vocabulary carries real meaning, and where the tools genuinely
        differ from one another. These are written from repeated hands-on use, and updated when the
        tools change — which, in this field, is often.
      </p>

      <h2>Corrections</h2>
      <p>
        If a prompt on this site does not reproduce, or a guide describes a parameter that has since
        changed, that is a bug and worth reporting. Write to
        <a href={`mailto:${SITE.email}`}>{SITE.email}</a> and the entry gets re-tested. Corrections
        are made on the page itself rather than quietly deleted, and substantive guide updates carry
        an updated date.
      </p>

      <h2>Who runs this</h2>
      <!-- TODO: replace with your name and a sentence of background. Readers and ad reviewers
           both look for a real person here; it is the single highest-value edit on this page. -->
      <p>
        trendingprompt is an independent project run by a single editor, not a company or a content
        farm. There is no team, no syndication, and no automated posting: every entry and every
        guide is written and checked by hand before it goes live.
      </p>

      <h2>How the site pays for itself</h2>
      <p>
        The site is free and has no accounts, no paywall, and no newsletter to sign up for. Running
        costs are covered by advertising. Ads, where present, are clearly distinguishable from
        editorial content, and no advertiser has any input into which prompts get published or how
        the tools are assessed. See the <a href="/privacy">privacy policy</a> for what advertising
        means for the data collected about your visit.
      </p>

      <h2>Contact</h2>
      <p>
        Corrections, takedown requests, and advertising enquiries all go to
        <a href={`mailto:${SITE.email}`}>{SITE.email}</a>. More detail on the
        <a href="/contact">contact page</a>.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>
</BaseLayout>
